   "t": 253.56666666666666,
   "title": "Glossary"
  }
 ],
 "narration": [
  {
   "id": "N01",
   "start": 0.6,
   "end": 8.333333333333334,
   "text": "A Japanese saw cuts as you pull it toward you. That one fact shapes much of the tool."
  },
  {
   "id": "N02",
   "start": 8.733333333333333,
   "end": 15.466666666666667,
   "text": "This time: the saws, and exactly where the cut goes in relation to the line."
  },
  {
   "id": "N03",
   "start": 21.266666666666666,
   "end": 37.06666666666667,
   "text": "Most Western saws cut on the push stroke. Japanese saws cut on the pull. Pulling puts the blade in tension, so it can be made thinner. A pushed blade generally needs to be stiffer, so that it doesn't buckle."
  },
  {
   "id": "N04",
   "start": 37.46666666666667,
   "end": 47.56666666666667,
   "text": "Every saw cut has a width, called the kerf. A thinner blade usually makes a narrower kerf, so there is less wood to remove."
  },
  {
   "id": "N05",
   "start": 51.766666666666666,
   "end": 74.03333333333333,
   "text": "A common carpenter's saw is the ryōba, which means double-edged. One edge rips, cutting along the grain; that's tatebiki. The other crosscuts, cutting across it; that's yokobiki. The design is fairly modern: it dates from the Meiji era, in the late eighteen hundreds."
  },
  {
   "id": "N06",
   "start": 77.73333333333333,
   "end": 92.86666666666666,
   "text": "Look closely at the teeth. Rip teeth work like a row of tiny chisels, paring along the fibers. Crosscut teeth are pointed like small knives, and sever the fibers one by one."
  },
  {
   "id": "N07",
   "start": 97.06666666666666,
   "end": 108.23333333333333,
   "text": "For fine joinery there is the dōzuki. Its blade is very thin, so a metal spine along the back keeps it stiff. It is classed as a crosscut saw."
  },
  {
   "id": "N08",
   "start": 108.63333333333334,
   "end": 121.36666666666666,
   "text": "Dōzuki is also the word for a tenon's shoulder, the face around its base. Cutting it is a clean crosscut: exactly the kind of work this saw does."
  },
  {
   "id": "N09",
   "start": 124.96666666666667,
   "end": 130.63333333333333,
   "text": "There is also the kataba, a saw with a single edge and no spine."
  },
  {
   "id": "N10",
   "start": 133.33333333333334,
   "end": 149.66666666666666,
   "text": "Look at the kerf end-on. The teeth are bent out alternately, left and right. This is called the set, or asari. So the kerf is a little wider than the blade, and that width is why the next question matters."
  },
  {
   "id": "N11",
   "start": 153.86666666666667,
   "end": 176.43333333333334,
   "text": "Where does the kerf go, relative to the line? The carpenter chooses, and the skills test manual sets out three options. In English shop talk: you can take the line, cutting it away; leave the line, sawing just on the waste side; or split it, with the kerf centered on the line."
  },
  {
   "id": "N12",
   "start": 176.83333333333334,
   "end": 184.7,
   "text": "The choice depends on how tight each joint should be. Some fits should be snug, and some a little looser."
  },
  {
   "id": "N13",
   "start": 189.4,
   "end": 208.46666666666667,
   "text": "The national skills test manual for carpenters gives an example. On one tenon, the thickness cuts split the line, so it fits a little looser. The width cuts leave the line, with the saw just on the waste side, so it fits tight."
  },
  {
   "id": "N14",
   "start": 208.86666666666667,
   "end": 215.03333333333333,
   "text": "Either way, the line is the reference. That's why the marking out had to be exact."
  },
  {
   "id": "N15",
   "start": 219.23333333333332,
   "end": 230.93333333333334,
   "text": "If you own Western saws, the same ideas carry over: rip and crosscut teeth, a backed saw for joinery. The big difference is the stroke."
  },
  {
   "id": "N16",
   "start": 233.73333333333332,
   "end": 239.33333333333334,
   "text": "Next time, the rest of the kit: the chisels, the hammer, and the plane."
  },
  {
   "id": "N17",
   "start": 240.33333333333334,
   "end": 253.06666666666666,
   "text": "Pull to cut. Rip along the grain, crosscut across it. And before every cut, decide: take the line, leave it, or split it."
  },
  {
   "id": "N18",
   "start": 262.96666666666664,
   "end": 272.6,
   "text": "One note: sharp-tool technique, and how to use these tools safely, is best learned from a teacher, at a bench, in person."
  }
 ],
